  "worked_solution": "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$",
